# Nmap Command Reference with Flag Explanations

## What is Nmap?

**Nmap (Network Mapper)** is an open-source network scanning and reconnaissance tool used to:

- Discover live hosts
- Scan ports
- Identify services
- Detect operating systems
- Perform vulnerability assessment
- Generate security reports

---

# Nmap Command Structure

```bash
nmap [flags/options] target
```

Example:

```bash
nmap -sV 192.168.1.10
```

Meaning:

```text
nmap     → Network scanning tool
-sV      → Service version detection
192.168.1.10 → Target IP address
```

---

# Common Nmap Flags

| Flag | Full Form | Purpose |
|--------|------------|----------|
| -sn | Ping Scan | Host discovery only |
| -sS | SYN Scan | Stealth TCP scan |
| -sT | TCP Connect Scan | Full TCP connection |
| -sU | UDP Scan | Scan UDP ports |
| -sV | Version Detection | Detect service versions |
| -O | OS Detection | Detect operating system |
| -A | Aggressive Scan | OS + Version + Scripts + Traceroute |
| -p | Port | Specify ports |
| -T4 | Timing Template | Faster scanning |
| -oN | Output Normal | Save results to text file |
| --script | NSE Script | Run Nmap scripts |
| --script=vuln | Vulnerability Scripts | Check for vulnerabilities |

